    "q-344484": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344484",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The members of a robotics club are testing a small robot in a courtyard lit by only one large lamp post.  Sam, a $1.4\\,\\text{m}$ student, is walking away from the post, and his shadow is increasing at a rate of $0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ If the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than  Sam, how fast is  Sam walking away from the post?\n\nSam's velocity is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given that the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than Sam, the height of the post is $7+1.4=8.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nLet's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/1c17902179842d470f95519599235a9998e4cfbe538a44c201e34c3627c493af.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is Sam's distance from the lamp post, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the length of Sam's shadow changes at the rate\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the rate of change of the length of Sam's shadow is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp post, and therefore, the length of the shadow *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{8.4} & =\\frac{y}{1.4} \\\\ 1.4x+1.4y & =8.4y \\\\ 1.4x & =7y \\\\ x & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5y) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\cdot (0.8) \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sam's velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
