      "source_example_id": "e-230",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles",
      "kp_id": "e-230"
    },
    "q-344428": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344428",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall student is jogging away from a $4.0\\,\\text{m}$ parking lot light at $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the length of the student’s shadow changing?\n\nThe length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/e500e84e039b0f8bef6afc7e636d6142309d65f13017c1f817fdf159b3f4d77c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the student’s distance from the parking lot light, while $y$ is the length of their shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the student’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the student’s velocity is *positive* because they are jogging *away* from the parking lot light, and therefore, the distance between the student and the light *increases* with time. \n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4.0} & =\\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\ 1.5x+1.5y & =4.0y \\\\ 1.5x & =2.5y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1.5}{2.5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{25}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot (2.4) \\\\  & ≈1.4\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately $1.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
