    "q-344508": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344508",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Ethan is walking toward a $360\\,\\text{in}$ light pole, which has a lamp on top. If Ethan's height is $72\\,\\text{in}$ and his shadow is changing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{in/s},$ how fast is he walking toward the light pole?\n\nEthan is moving at a speed of \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in/s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/4e82a1365099bcf76ae91b75d0d1ad276e4c35f8b172fc60b4d22e9156128d7e.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance from Ethan to the light pole, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that Ethan's shadow's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that Ethan's shadow's velocity is *negative* because he is walking *toward* the light pole, and therefore the distance between him and the light pole *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{360} & =\\frac{y}{72} \\\\ 72x+72y & =360y \\\\ 72x & =288y \\\\ x & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4(-6)=-24\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ethan's speed is  $24\\,\\text{in/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
