    "q-344494": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344494",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "A bright lantern $L$ is on the ground $18\\,\\text{ft}$ away from a wall and a $6\\,\\text{ft}$ tall actor is moving towards the wall at a rate of $3\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the actor is $8\\,\\text{ft}$ from the lantern?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14209554/assets/0a47e801db651c027415a7943e5327bd378c1a08e533dc69673ab4304c8cc7b4.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the actor’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the actor’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the actor’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(18-x)+x} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{18} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{108}{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{108}{18-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{(18-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=18-8=10\\,\\text{ft}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
